{"name":"Seamount","hex":"#15646d","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"seamount","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An underwater mountain with a prominence of more than 1,000 meters, which does not breach the surface of the water."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/seamount"}],"entries":[{"title":"Seamount","blocks":[{"text":"A seamount is a large submarine landform that rises from the ocean floor without reaching the water surface, and thus is not an island, islet, or cliff-rock. Seamounts are typically formed from extinct volcanoes that rise abruptly and are usually found rising from the seafloor to 100–4,000 m (330–13,120 ft) in height. They are defined by oceanographers as independent features that rise to at least 1,000 m (3,281 ft) above the seafloor, characteristically of conical form. The peaks are often found hundreds to thousands of meters below the surface, and are therefore considered to be within the deep sea. During their evolution over geologic time, the largest seamounts may reach the sea surface, where wave action erodes the summit to form a flat surface. After they have subsided and sunk below the sea surface, such flat-top seamounts are called \"guyots\" or \"tablemounts\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/68/Bear_Seamount.jpg/330px-Bear_Seamount.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seamount"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/SeamontDavidson_expedition_bathymetric-2002.jpg/500px-SeamontDavidson_expedition_bathymetric-2002.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/50/SeamontDavidson_expedition_bathymetric-2002.jpg/500px-SeamontDavidson_expedition_bathymetric-2002.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Bathymetric mapping of part of Davidson Seamount. The dots indicate significant coral nurseries.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASeamontDavidson_expedition_bathymetric-2002.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Distribution_of_seamounts_and_guyots_in_the_North_Pacific.pdf/page1-500px-Distribution_of_seamounts_and_guyots_in_the_North_Pacific.pdf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Distribution_of_seamounts_and_guyots_in_the_North_Pacific.pdf/page1-1280px-Distribution_of_seamounts_and_guyots_in_the_North_Pacific.pdf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d1/Distribution_of_seamounts_and_guyots_in_the_North_Pacific.pdf/page1-500px-Distribution_of_seamounts_and_guyots_in_the_North_Pacific.pdf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Distribution of seamounts and guyots in the North Pacific","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADistribution_of_seamounts_and_guyots_in_the_North_Pacific.pdf"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/N_Atlantic_seamounts_%28Converted%29.pdf/page1-500px-N_Atlantic_seamounts_%28Converted%29.pdf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/N_Atlantic_seamounts_%28Converted%29.pdf/page1-1280px-N_Atlantic_seamounts_%28Converted%29.pdf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5a/N_Atlantic_seamounts_%28Converted%29.pdf/page1-500px-N_Atlantic_seamounts_%28Converted%29.pdf.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Distribution of seamounts and guyots in the North Atlantic","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AN_Atlantic_seamounts_(Converted).pdf"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Pillow_basalt_crop_l.jpg/500px-Pillow_basalt_crop_l.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Pillow_basalt_crop_l.jpg/1280px-Pillow_basalt_crop_l.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/08/Pillow_basalt_crop_l.jpg/500px-Pillow_basalt_crop_l.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Pillow lava, a type of basalt flow that originates from lava-water interactions during submarine eruptions[13]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3APillow_basalt_crop_l.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Diurnal_Flow_over_a_Ridge_-_NOAA-PMEL.gif/250px-Diurnal_Flow_over_a_Ridge_-_NOAA-PMEL.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/b/bd/Diurnal_Flow_over_a_Ridge_-_NOAA-PMEL.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/bd/Diurnal_Flow_over_a_Ridge_-_NOAA-PMEL.gif/250px-Diurnal_Flow_over_a_Ridge_-_NOAA-PMEL.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Animations depicting current flow over seamounts and ridges.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADiurnal_Flow_over_a_Ridge_-_NOAA-PMEL.gif"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Bubblegum_coral_on_davidson.jpg/330px-Bubblegum_coral_on_davidson.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/15/Bubblegum_coral_on_davidson.jpg/330px-Bubblegum_coral_on_davidson.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Grenadier fish (Coryphaenoides sp.) and bubblegum coral (Paragorgia arborea) on the crest of Davidson Seamount. These are two species attracted to the seamount; Paragorgia arborea in particular grows in the surrounding area as well, but nowhere near as profusely.[19]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABubblegum_coral_on_davidson.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Orange_roughy.png/500px-Orange_roughy.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2e/Orange_roughy.png/500px-Orange_roughy.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Because of overfishing at their seamount spawning grounds, stocks of orange roughy (Hoplostethus atlanticus) have plummeted; experts say that it could take decades for the species to restore itself to its former numbers.[29]","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AOrange_roughy.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Koral1.jpg/500px-Koral1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fc/Koral1.jpg/500px-Koral1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Coral earrings of this type are often made from coral harvested off seamounts.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AKoral1.jpg"}],"sounds":{}}}